    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media of all political stripes agree that the statutory pension fund is heading for a significant shortfall as the population ages, and that this fiscal pressure will require either higher contributions or a greater role for capital‑based components such as the Riester or new private pension accounts. Across the spectrum there is a shared assessment that the “Rente mit 63” creates a heavy intergenerational cost, quantified in the headlines as tens of thousands of euros per younger worker, and therefore must be curtailed or restructured. At the same time, most outlets converge on the expectation that statutory pensions will be adjusted upward by roughly four percent in 2027, reflecting the legally mandated increase mechanism.",
      "controversial_points": "The debate pits intergenerational equity against fiscal sustainability, with younger voters fearing higher contributions for older retirees. Conservatives emphasize protecting pension reserves and limiting early retirement, while left‑wing parties demand expanded benefits and stronger redistribution. Liberals push for capital‑funded solutions and market participation, confronting skeptics who view them as risky and socially unjust. Far‑right narratives frame the system as a betrayal, linking pension cuts to broader anti‑elitist and nationalist grievances."
